// std.memory: where a value lives and whether two names are one object.
//
// C's &x takes an address and *p reads what is there. Marslang has the first
// half only: an address is for telling objects apart and for debugging output,
// never for reaching back into memory. Values are freed automatically, so
// nothing here is needed to write a correct program.
takepkg rs.memory = native;
takepkg std.Decorator;

@Decorator.docstring("""
    The address of a value, as a longint.

        a = arr(1, 2);
        out(memory.address(a));   // 2338294874432

    Strings, arrays, sets, maps, pairs, instances, functions, and packages live
    behind a pointer and have one. A number, a boolean, or null is stored
    inside the value itself, like a C value held in a register, and raises
    TypeError.

    An address means nothing between two runs, and once an object is freed the
    same number can come back for a different one. Never store it; to compare
    identity use same().
""")
func address(any value) => native.address(value);

@Decorator.docstring("""
    The address of a value as text, for printing: the kind, @, and the address
    in hexadecimal, as in array@0x22056ab1c40. Values without an address raise
    TypeError, as with address().
""")
func pointer(any value) => native.pointer(value);

@Decorator.docstring("""
    Whether two names hold one object, rather than two that look alike.

        a = arr(1, 2);
        out(memory.same(a, a), memory.same(a, arr(1, 2)));   // true false
        out(memory.same("ab", "ab"));   // whether that is one string or two

    Numbers, booleans, and null have no identity to compare, so for them this
    is ordinary equality, and comparing one with an object is false.
""")
func same(any a, any b) => native.same(a, b);

@Decorator.docstring("""
    How many references the interpreter holds to a value's object.

        a = arr(1, 2);
        b = a;
        out(memory.refs(a));   // more than one: a and b share the array

    This is why a value is still alive. The exact number counts references the
    interpreter makes while running the call, so compare numbers rather than
    reading one on its own. Values without an address raise TypeError.
""")
func refs(any value) => native.refs(value);

@Decorator.docstring("""
    Free objects that only refer to each other and return how many were freed.

    Marslang frees a value as soon as nothing refers to it; a cycle, such as an
    array holding itself, needs this collector, which also runs on its own as a
    program allocates. Calling it is a diagnostic, not something a correct
    program needs.
""")
func collect() => native.collect();
